  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a west-southwest flow aloft prevailing\nacross the Western High Plains. Near the surface, a weak area \nof low pressure remains anchored in southeast Colorado.\n\nDespite a relatively dry pattern persisting early in the \nperiod, there are minimal chances (20-40%) for showers and \npossibly an isolated thunderstorm here or there late today/early\nWednesday as the SREF points to another upper level shortwave \ntrough lifting northeast through the Southern Rockies early this\nmorning, and farther into the high plains of eastern Colorado \nthis afternoon. Near the surface, modest moisture return at best\nwill push surface dewpoints up into 50s(F) across \nsouthwest/south central Kansas, introducing some meager \ninstability with MUCAPE values pushing 500 J/kg or so by late \nafternoon. Meanwhile, an attendant frontal boundary to the \norganizing low in southeast Colorado is projected to \ndevelop/extend east-northeast through western Kansas by early \nevening. Although dynamic support aloft will remain less than \nfavorable, steepening low/mid-level lapse rates combined with \nvery marginal instability will set the stage for shower/isolated\nthunderstorm development across central Kansas and portions of \nsouthwest Kansas as H5 vort maxima transition through the high \nplains, interacting with the aforementioned boundary. The best \nchance for isolated showers/storm or two initially will be \nacross extreme southwest Kansas late afternoon/early evening as \nsuggested by some CAMs and the HREF indicating a 20-30% \nprobability of 6-hr QPF exceeding a mere 0.1 of an inch by late \nevening with that probability then shifting to central Kansas \novernight. What little precip chances exist dissipate Wednesday \nas a developing upper level ridge amplifies across the Western \nHigh Plains.\n\nAn ongoing warming trend continues today as prevailing\nsoutherlies help draw slightly warmer air into the area, \npushing H85 temperatures well above 10C in central Kansas to \nnear 17-18C in extreme southwest Kansas. With the HREF showing a\n40-60% probability of temperatures exceeding 70F in \ncentral/south central Kansas to better than a 90% probability of\ntopping 70F elsewhere, expect widespread afternoon highs in the\n70s(F). Look for similar highs Wednesday.",
